{0 Stage 1 Phase Facts Audit}

This page audits the Rocq use of [pssEnsures], [kairosPhaseClause], and
[FactPhaseFormula]. It follows the arbitration rule from
{!page-stage1_ocaml_rocq_arbitration}: do not change OCaml merely to mimic a
Rocq proof artifact; first identify the mathematical role.

{1 Question}

Does Rocq really need every [pssEnsures] formula to be a phase fact, or should
Rocq distinguish step-contract postconditions from product/phase propagation
facts?

Current answer:

{[
Rocq currently maps every pssEnsures formula to a phase clause, but the
soundness proof uses pssEnsures primarily as safe-step postconditions. Only
specific subfamilies of ensures are needed to reconstruct next-tick
propagation facts.
]}

Therefore the direction is {b OCaml -> Rocq}: Rocq should be refined so that
postconditions and phase/propagation facts are separate notions, or at least
so that phase clauses range over an explicit phase-fact subset rather than
all [pssEnsures].

{1 Rocq Construction}

In [theories/Compilation/Stage1/Construction/SummaryGeneration.v],
[kairosSummary] builds [ensures_h] from four components:

- safe-case guarantee guards;
- the disjunction of safe-case guards;
- shifted destination state invariants;
- guarded destination phase formulas
  [kairosHistImp guard phase].

It then does:

{[
phase_clauses := map (kairosPhaseClause step base_hypotheses) ensures_h
pssEnsures := ensures_h
]}

So every safe-step postcondition becomes both:

- a Stage 2 safe-contract ensure, through [pssEnsures];
- a Stage 1 phase clause, through [kairosPhaseClause].

This is exactly the conflation under audit.

{1 Classification Use}

[kairosSummaryFamilies] classifies a phase clause by shape:

{[
isPhaseClause pss kc :=
  In kc pss.(pssClauses) /\ kairosClauseIsPhase kc = true
]}

and [kairosClauseIsPhase] only checks whether the clause conclusions contain a
[FactPhaseFormula].

The only direct uses found for [isPhaseClause], [FactPhaseFormula], and
[summaryClauseFamiliesWf] are structural well-formedness/classification
proofs:

- [kairosSummaryFamiliesWfAny] in
  [theories/Compilation/Stage1/Construction/SummaryShapes.v];
- the generic pass contracts in
  [theories/Obligations/Generation/GeneratedObligations.v].

I did not find a run-level soundness step that consumes [isPhaseClause] itself
to prove automata validity. The run-level proof consumes generated safe
obligations and selected [pssEnsures] members.

{1 Soundness Use}

The semantic use of [pssEnsures] is in
[theories/Soundness/Compiler/CompilerSoundness.v]:

{[
safeTripletOfSummary ps pss
  postcondition:
    forall h, In h (lowerDedupEnsures pss.(pssEnsures)) -> h holds
]}

Then [kairosGeneratedSafeEnsuresMemberHolds] gives access to one proved
ensure of a safe generated contract.

The predecessor witness is isolated in
[theories/Soundness/RequiresPropagation/RequiresPropagationSafePredecessor.v].
It reconstructs the safe predecessor at tick [k] and attaches it to one
generated safe contract.  Low-level facts are kept at their semantic layer:
transition-selection inversion is in the instrumented step semantics,
product-state live/safe facts are in Stage1 product-step construction, and
run/product-anchor bridges shared by soundness proofs are isolated in
[theories/Soundness/RunStepFacts.v].  Singleton step-contract anchor facts stay
in [theories/Obligations/Proofs/TripletValidity.v], because they only describe
the generic shape of [stepContractAnchorHolds] and [ProductStepAnchor] field
equality.  Summary/contract step-list
adaptation stays in [theories/Soundness/GeneratedContracts/GeneratedContractsDefs.v],
where [summaryAnchorHolds] is defined.
The main transport lemma
[kairosSrcInvAndPropagationHoldNextTickFromSafe] in
[theories/Soundness/RequiresPropagation/RequiresPropagationSharedStep.v]
then uses this mechanism for two concrete subfamilies:

- shifted destination state invariants, via
  [kairosGroupedSafeDirectMemberShiftedDstInvInv];
- safe-case guarantee guards, via
  [kairosGroupedSafeDirectMemberSafeCaseInEnsuresInv].

The proof then transports these postconditions across ticks to reconstruct:

- destination state invariants at the next source state;
- propagation requirements built from incoming safe guards.

This is a postcondition/transport story. It does not require every ensure to
be classified as a phase clause.

{1 Guarded Destination Phase}

An earlier Rocq version also proved that guarded destination phase formulas
were present in [pssEnsures], through a dedicated invariant and its
singleton/merge/insert preservation lemmas.

The audit found no current run-level soundness use of this guarded phase
subfamily. Since it did not contribute to a final result, this predicate and
its preservation lemmas were removed from the main Rocq development. Guarded
destination phase formulas may still be generated as postconditions, but Rocq
does not maintain a separate invariant family for them until a final theorem
actually consumes it.

{1 OCaml Comparison}

OCaml currently separates these roles more clearly:

- [Post.run_node] adds step-contract postconditions to [summary.ensures]:
  safe disjunctions, destination invariants, guarded destination invariants,
  and product-characteristic preservation facts;
- [Kernel_clause_projection.build] emits phase-related classified clauses
  from guarantee guards and compatibility/source-phase formulas;
- it does not classify every [summary.ensures] formula as a phase fact.

This is conceptually healthier. A local postcondition may be useful for Why3
and for transport, but it is not automatically a product phase fact.

{1 Decision}

Do not change OCaml to emit a [PhaseStepSummary] for every
[summary.ensures] formula.

The right alignment direction is:

1. refine the Rocq paper model so [pssEnsures] remains the Stage 2
   postcondition set;
2. introduce, if needed, a separate Stage 1 phase-fact set or predicate;
3. state which postcondition subfamilies are sufficient for propagation:
   safe-case guards and shifted destination invariants are currently used;
4. keep guarded destination phase formulas separate until a proof actually
   consumes them at run level;
5. only then decide whether [Canonical_obligations.stage1] needs an explicit
   phase-fact field.

{1 Rocq Status}

The Rocq development now names only the part of this distinction consumed by
the final proof, without changing the final theorems or the
[ProofStepSummary] record:

- [kairosGroupedSafeSummaryCoreProgressionFactsInv] groups the two
  postcondition subfamilies currently consumed by next-tick propagation:
  safe-case guards and shifted destination state invariants.

The next-tick propagation proof now consumes the core predicate explicitly:
[kairosSrcInvAndPropagationHoldNextTickFromSafe] obtains
[kairosGroupedSafeSummaryCoreProgressionFactsInv] once for the safe
predecessor summary, then uses its two components to recover shifted
destination invariants and safe-case guards.

This is intentionally not an implementation certification layer.  It is the
Rocq/paper vocabulary for the proof principle: safe-step postconditions remain
the contract facts, and the only named progression facts in Rocq are the
subfamilies needed by a final soundness argument.

{1 Dead Proof Surface Audit}

The Rocq cleanup follows this rule: a named fact belongs to the main
formalization only when it contributes to a final soundness result or to a
lemma consumed by such a result. Otherwise it must either be local proof
structure or be removed.

The audit removed the following unused proof families:

- the guarded destination phase invariant family, which was preserved through
  grouping but consumed by no final run-level theorem;
- the generic grouped-safe-summary invariant, which only propagated inside
  grouping modules and had no soundness consumer;
- unused wrapper lemmas for shifted destination invariants and safe-case
  guards in grouped summaries;
- unused shape wrappers in [SummaryShapes];
- the public unsafe-case coverage branch in [GroupingConstruction], whose
  live analogue is a local proof in [SummaryGeneration];
- one dead helper in generated-contract origins.

The audit also reduced the public proof surface by making helper lemmas local
when they are used only to establish the next exported result. The remaining
grouping invariants are all consumed by [Soundness] or by witnesses used by
[Soundness]: identity, canonical requires, source liveness, safe-case guards,
shifted destination invariants, and the core progression predicate.

The second cleanup pass also factored duplicated live facts into the Stage 1
grouping layer, [GroupingCorrectness]. This layer now owns the construction
proofs that start from relevant singleton summaries and lift them through
grouping. Its facts cover:

- identity invariants for relevant singleton summaries;
- identity invariants for direct grouped safe summaries;
- canonical-requires invariants for relevant singleton summaries;
- canonical-requires invariants for direct grouped safe summaries.

This removed repeated local proofs from generated-contract origins, generated
contract class facts, safe requires propagation, compiler soundness, and the
unsafe contradiction kernel while keeping the final theorem statements
unchanged.

A third cleanup pass factored lookup facts for [kairosFindBadStepForDst] into
[ContractExtraction]. Successful lookup inversion and witness completeness are
properties of the Stage 2 extraction function itself, not local facts of
generated-contract origins, concrete Stage 2 packaging, or the unsafe
contradiction kernel.

The same pass moved [kairosCollectUnsafeContractsContainsCase] to
[ContractExtraction]. Membership in the collected unsafe-contract list is a
property of the collector, not a duplicated proof obligation of each consumer.

A fourth cleanup pass moved the remaining live grouped-summary facts out of
requires propagation and into [GroupingCorrectness]: source liveness,
safe-case guards in ensures, shifted destination invariants, and the core
progression predicate. The requires-propagation modules now keep only the
local witness-packaging facts that are specific to their temporal proof.

A fifth cleanup pass audited [MainSoundness]. The final theorem no longer
owns elementary facts about bad-state booleans, product-step classification,
summary relevance, or selected-transition inversion. These facts now live in
the lower layers that define the corresponding notions: [AnchorsAndInvariants],
[SummaryGeneration], and the instrumented step semantics. [MainSoundness]
keeps only the final one-step contradiction and induction structure.

A sixth cleanup pass audited generated-contract origins. Unsafe-case origin
through safe-summary grouping is now stated in Stage1 as
[kairosGroupSafeSingletonsUnsafeCaseOrigin], because it is a property of the
grouping construction rather than of generated contracts. The origins module
keeps only the local record-shape equality needed to identify one generated
bad contract with [kairosBadContractOfSummaryStep].

The same pass moved bad-guarantee summary coverage from [SummaryGeneration]
to [GroupingConstruction]. The constructor file still defines summaries and
singleton membership, while the proof that grouped Stage1 summaries cover an
input step/unsafe-case pair now lives with the other grouping coverage facts.
The safe-case and unsafe-case coverage proofs now share one local generic
proof parameterized by the case-list projection; the public API remains
specialized as [kairosGroupSafeSingletonsCoversInputStepAndSafeCaseWithIdentity]
and [kairosGroupSafeSingletonsCoversInputStepAndUnsafeCaseWithIdentity].

The witness pass follows the same pattern.  A local generic
[summaryCaseWitnessInv] proof handles preservation through
merge/insert/fold/grouping, while the witness-facing predicates remain
specialized as safe-case and unsafe-case witness invariants.

The exported witness surface was then audited.  The intermediate witness
invariants are local proof structure.  The remaining non-local facts are
exactly the bridge facts consumed outside the witness module: concrete
safe-step recovery for requires propagation, unsafe-case bad-step recovery
for Stage 2/unsafe contradiction, summary well-formedness for generated
contract origins, and the grouped-summary membership fact used by generated
contract class facts.

Imports were pruned accordingly: [SafeUnsafeWitnesses] is no longer imported
by modules that only needed generic Stage 1 or Stage 2 construction facts.
The remaining imports are direct consumers of one of the exported bridge
facts above.

The same import audit was applied to the Stage 1 grouping modules.  Consumers
now import only the grouping layer they actually use:
[GroupedSummaryInvariants] for invariant predicates that are unfolded in
soundness proofs, [GroupingConstruction] for coverage/origin bridge facts, and
[GroupingCorrectness] only where member-level preservation lemmas are consumed.
[GroupedSummaryInvariants] now only defines the invariant predicates; singleton
bases were moved into [GroupingCorrectness], because they are proof structure
for the grouping algorithm rather than standalone mathematical vocabulary.
The temporary [GeneratedContractsGroupingFacts] module was removed: its facts
were Stage 1 grouping facts, not generated-contract facts. Modules that only
needed generic summaries or concrete stages no longer import the whole
grouping stack.

Inside [GroupingCorrectness], fold-preservation helpers are local, as are the
intermediate direct safe-case and shifted-invariant facts used only to build
the exported core progression fact.  The public surface now consists of the
member/direct bridge facts that are consumed outside the module.

The lower grouping layers were tightened as well.  [GroupingKeys] now exposes
only insert-preservation lemmas; merge-preservation facts are local helpers
for proving insertion.  [GroupingConstruction] no longer imports invariant or
key-preservation layers, because coverage/origin construction only depends on
summary construction and identity equality.

The final import pass applied the same principle across the Stage 2 and
Soundness modules.  Files now import [ContractExtraction], [TransportPrevToStep],
[TransportStepToPost], [ShiftBackward], [LoweringSemantics], and concrete stage
packaging only when they call a fact from that layer directly.  This keeps the
Rocq code organized by proof role:

- invariant definitions;
- semantic transition/product-state facts;
- singleton step-contract anchor facts;
- product-step structural equality facts;
- summary/contract anchor adaptation facts;
- run-step bridge facts for soundness;
- grouping construction and grouping correctness;
- Stage1-to-Stage2 witness extraction;
- safe-predecessor extraction for next-tick propagation;
- StepContract extraction and temporal transport;
- Soundness assembly.

The latest cleanup pass made the proof packaging explicit at the remaining
crossing points without changing definitions:

- [GroupingConstruction] names the left/right unsafe-case origin packaging used
  by fold grouping;
- [SummaryShapes] names the component package for safe singleton summaries;
- [SafeUnsafeWitnesses] factors the inversion from relevant singleton summaries
  to their generating product-transition components;
- [RequiresPropagationSharedBase] consumes the named product-step anchor
  projection from [TripletValidity] instead of destructuring the anchor shape
  directly;
- [ContractExtraction] owns the bad-step lookup packaging and [Concrete] uses
  the successful-lookup inversion once, then reuses the projected facts.

No final theorem statement was changed by this cleanup.

{1 Consequence For Completeness}

This is not a weakening of the proof principle. It removes a conflation:

- completeness of local deductive obligations should be about proving the
  necessary postconditions and unsafe exclusions;
- phase/source facts should be the facts required to progress the product
  proof;
- backend grouping or clause classification should not decide which formulas
  are semantically phase facts.

The completeness story is cleaner if Rocq states exactly which generated facts
are needed for progression, instead of making every postcondition a phase
formula by construction.

{1 Next Work}

The next concrete step is to decide how much of this vocabulary should appear
in the paper calculus:

- leave [pssEnsures] as the safe-contract postcondition set;
- present core progression facts as the postconditions needed by the
  next-instant proof;
- present phase facts as a separate optional/product-state family;
- keep [SummaryClauseFamilies] as a classifier over generated clauses, and
  avoid defining phase clauses as "all ensured formulas" in the paper model.
